ustom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2950"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-10-15T05:41:53-07:00","id":1418346,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>cKnight</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/trinity/1418279-question-on-a-change-in-combat-mechanics#post1418279\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So would it change the system to much if you separated the enhancements for damage/power from accuracy/advantage?<br /> <br /> Ex:. Roll to attack, add enhancements that contribute to advantage or accuracy. If you buy off defense (1 plus defensive roll, dodgy edges ect..), then add damage/power enhancements and the targets soft armor/durability. Do simple math and have your result.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> That's basically how it works in the book.<br /> <br /> Roll to attack, add all enhancements. If you buy off defense, compare the remaining to soft armor/durability. There are a few effects which bypass soft armor or reduce it, which are kind of like damage only enhancements.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8719-florin","name":"Florin","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"940"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}